  "principal_findings_closed": [
    "A strictly newer execution generation is minted only after an explicit trusted death witness; former holder FlowRun, TaskRun, metadata, stream and signed-event writes are rejected before mutation.",
    "Recovery preparation uses normal CoreTaskPolicy transitions, preserves TaskRun identity and retry budget, and records an immutable exact-replay outcome.",
    "Recorder admission binds generation, holder, FlowRun run count, TaskRun run count, immutable manifest identity/policy/tags, reserved payload fields and durable EOF under database locks.",
    "Manifest seal and recovery all-or-none SQL CHECKs explicitly reject near-complete NULL witnesses on PostgreSQL and SQLite.",
    "Cross-origin redirects do not receive execution credentials; legacy direct HTTP paths and current clients both carry scoped credentials only to the authorized origin.",
    "Paired PostgreSQL and SQLite authority migrations have normalized parity, adversarial upgrade/downgrade tests and recorded migration boundaries."
  ],
